00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.020
And sorry if it's
a repeat for folks

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.690
who have seen this before, but
it all starts, in our mind,

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:02.850
with the digital value chain,
so where we really focus on what

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.790
are we delivering for the
developers in two phases.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.280
One, it's an API
for the developer

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.070
who's building an application.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.810
Or on the other side,
it's the developer

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.320
or the development team
that is producing the APIs.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.930
And we've got to make sure
that those two constituents are

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.660
really, really happy
with the end result.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.550
So what we have here
in this value chain

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:28.440
is to really make sure that the
balance is between those two

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.320
teams, or those
two constituents,

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.470
but more importantly, the
contract between those two

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.120
is just via an API.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.730
And the reason I use the
word contract, again,

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:43.120
is that it has to be very
clear to the developer, who's

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:46.020
consuming the API,
what they're getting,

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.570
and how the API will
behave when they're

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:50.040
building the application.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.350
And if we can't
maintain that contract,

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.510
then you lose the
trust and you also

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.300
create a ton of issues for that
application and the end user

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.710
who is eventually going
to use the application.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:04.610
So in this case, for us, the
security and how you implement

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.980
the security is part
of the contract.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.920
So if you don't think about
that as part of the contract,

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.720
what you can't do
is deliver an API

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.960
and suddenly go and change
it because you suddenly

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.779
have a new security
implementation or a need

00:04:18.779 --> 00:04:21.990
and decided to change the
experience for the developer,

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:24.690
and ultimately, it
impacts the end user

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:26.350
who is using the application.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:28.200
So when you are
building this API

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.970
and when you're
publishing this API,

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:34.080
you have to think about
security as part of the contract

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:37.520
and what else you think
is part of the contract.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.190
For the API producing
team, I think

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:42.050
this is, again, in
terms of making sure

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:45.870
that while you create the API
which is very easy to use,

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.970
you have the right tooling built
in so that you can monitor,

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.350
you can analyze, and you
can do the right things

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:54.830
without affecting the developer
or surprising the developer.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:57.140
So that's kind of the
other aspect of how

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.480
we want to think about this.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:02.450
Now, as you're
building applications,

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:04.850
what your really end goal
is to really make sure

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:07.919
that we're building awesome
experiences for the customers.

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:09.710
You want to make sure
that the developer is

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:14.090
able to build the applications
faster and integrate faster.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:15.980
The developer
who's using the API

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:19.140
needs to be onboarded
in an easy fashion.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.900
So when you think
about onboarding,

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.540
it is also about making
sure that onboarding

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.050
happens in a secure way.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.020
So for example, in
the developer portal,

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.240
you could say, come and
test and play with this API,

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.001
and you don't need
a key, but you

00:05:32.001 --> 00:05:33.500
are going to be
rate limited, you're

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.990
going to have a sandbox
environment with mock data,

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:38.600
so you go at it, no problem.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.000
But when it comes to
actual production APIs,

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.250
then you may want to
do things differently.

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:46.340
And you may implement a
set of different policies

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:48.410
which are to protect
your backend,

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:49.910
make sure that the
application does

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:52.760
a great experience
for the customer,

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.330
and it's very performant.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:59.160
The API itself is a gateway to
your backend system so it does

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.359
need protection.

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:01.900
And while you're
doing that, you also

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:03.858
want to make sure that
there's a consistent way

00:06:03.858 --> 00:06:06.400
to protect the APIs so
that each developer who's

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.220
producing the API
from the API team

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:11.512
is not figuring
it out themselves.

00:06:11.512 --> 00:06:12.970
So part of today's
session is going

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:15.850
to be how do we let you
have a consistent way

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:18.460
to reuse some of these
kind of security policies?

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.740
And that's what
we're going to do.

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:23.950
On the other side, you
do want the automation

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.800
available to your API
teams to, again, use

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.680
our APIs for the
product itself to do

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:33.390
what you need to do in terms of
building new versions of APIs,

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:35.600
or releasing new
versions of APIs,

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.900
or implementing security
policies on the APIs--

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:39.610
again, at scale.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:41.710
And the goal here is
not to do it one-by-one.

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:43.480
If you have a large
set of APIs, we

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:45.732
should give you the
automation to do that.

00:06:45.732 --> 00:06:47.440
And then, of course,
your backend systems

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.200
are, again, I'm sure,
protected by themselves,

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:54.440
but this is one more layer of
security that we can offer you.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:57.620
Now, as we think through all
the security implications

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.100
and reusability today, one
of the things we want to do

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.310
is really think about it
from a holistic API lifecycle

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:05.390
perspective.

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:07.430
So we talk about this a
lot with our customers

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:10.310
because it's not about
just exposing the API

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:11.380
or consuming the API.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:14.270
It is really thinking
about this in a much more

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:15.710
holistic fashion.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:18.380
And it all starts with
the API design itself.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:21.470
So as some of you might
have heard from Marsha

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:23.870
in the previous session,
we are a big supporter

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:27.872
and we're part of the
OpenAPI Spec community.

00:07:27.872 --> 00:07:29.830
And we really want to
contribute to making sure

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:33.980
that's the best open standard
for designing and creating

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:35.000
APIs.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.980
So it starts with
the design aspect.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:39.320
And we allow you to then
do interesting things

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:42.920
in our product to create the
right kind of API endpoints,

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.560
and then make sure that there's
enough security around it,

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:46.970
and then publish it.

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:48.470
So at this point,
what you've done

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:51.200
is you've taken a
very well-designed API

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:53.630
and then constructed
the right security

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:56.136
policies and the
right experience,

00:07:56.136 --> 00:07:57.260
and then publishing it out.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:07:59.360
And what does
publishing it out mean?

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:02.540
Publishing means it'll go to the
developer portal or someplace

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:05.000
where the consumers
of these APIs

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.790
can come and read,
and engage with you,

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:09.560
and consume those APIs.

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:11.900
Now, while they do that,
there's other aspects

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:14.191
of things which you need to
start thinking about, which

00:08:14.191 --> 00:08:17.360
is that, how do I scale the
experience for those APIs?

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:20.510
Sometimes this could be things
like you have a store location

00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:21.410
API.

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:24.110
And we've seen this in terms
of store location, content,

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:26.900
and things like that,
where our product is

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:30.440
able to actually cache and
respond to the API requests

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:33.429
faster without you
having to go re-platform

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:38.030
or re-architect what you have
in the backend and scale that.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:40.610
So there are certain use cases
we won't be able to cache,

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:43.159
but most of the times, we are
able to do that kind of work,

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:46.940
allowing you to scale the API
consumption without having

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:49.490
to go and touch an
old legacy system

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:52.590
and try to scale that
at this point in time.

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:55.520
Or look at monitoring
these APIs usage,

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.220
and then decide what do you
want to do in terms of behavior

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.800
change, or performance
change, or even

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.770
think about systems which
are not being used up much,

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:05.780
and then make the right
decisions for that.

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:08.080
That comes with the
monitoring aspect

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.320
and with the analytic aspect.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.000
So monitoring also gives you
things like is my API up?

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:16.130
Is there any latency
that's increasing suddenly?

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:17.960
How do I think about that?

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:20.040
And go and solve for that.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:21.880
Last, but not the
least, in some cases,

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:23.880
you may want to go
and monetize the APIs

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.220
because it's the right way
to build an extra channel

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:28.260
or distribution model.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:31.860
And when you do that, you're
trying and creating a new way

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:35.190
to open up your services to a
different set of customers who

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:37.500
are not engaging
with you previously,

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.800
or you're engaging with a
set of customers who actually

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.410
are doing it today, but
probably not in the most

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:44.500
efficient fashion.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:46.590
So we have customers
today, for example, they

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:51.240
go on a one-on-one integration
with certain partners.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:54.210
And it is almost
hand-coded, very

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:57.720
specific integrations that
take months to deliver.

00:09:57.720 --> 00:09:59.670
And anytime you change
something on your side

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:03.090
in terms of the API, they go
and do that all over again.

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:06.120
The idea is to keep the
business relationship as such,

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:09.120
but try and improve the speed
at which you can innovate

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:11.190
and improve the experience
for that partner

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:13.230
and their customers
for the services

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.840
they offer from your side.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.780
So this whole lifecycle
is extremely important

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:22.470
when it comes to thinking about
the security and the policies

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:25.160
and reusability because it's
not a one-time exercise.

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:28.237
So you want to think
about this at every stage.

00:10:28.237 --> 00:10:29.820
And part of our
session today is going

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:32.400
to be thinking through
some of these aspects

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:35.430
and walking through
a real example.

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:39.665
So let's look at the three
things we will cover today.

00:10:39.665 --> 00:10:41.040
And again, we are
not going to go

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:42.730
through each one
of these examples,

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:44.700
but I wanted to kind
of lay it out for you

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:46.740
so that you at least can
have a sense in terms

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:50.060
of what we are trying to do
when we talk about reusability

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:51.860
and consistency.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:53.390
We talked a lot about security.

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:56.990
And we do have a ton of
features and benefits for you

00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:01.130
in the product for security,
starting with API Keys, OAuth.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:04.060
We just announced SAML
integration for authentication.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:05.630
We'll have Role-Based
Access Control

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:07.430
for a lot of things
in our product.

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:12.560
We have a set of very specific
policies for threat prediction.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.040
And then we also have
something for bot detection

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:17.320
and prevention.

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:20.470
All these security features
are available to you.

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:22.720
And you can decide
how you want to apply

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:24.850
them, depending on
the use cases you have

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.560
and when you want to do it
for a certain API, APIs,

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:29.620
and who the consumers are.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:32.200
The second thing is
about visibility,

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:34.960
because people, once
they start using APIs,

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:37.210
as an API, what
you want to do is

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:40.510
look at who is using my APIs,
how often they're using it,

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:42.460
what are the typical
patterns of usage,

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:44.650
is there something else I
should be doing in the API

00:11:44.650 --> 00:11:47.230
to make life easier
for those customers.

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:48.610
That's something you want to do.

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:49.985
But along with
that, you are also

00:11:49.985 --> 00:11:53.870
doing something operationally,
looking at things like--

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:56.200
things are going not
well, looking at the logs.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.030
You're looking at
analytics and looking

00:11:58.030 --> 00:12:01.380
at things like, where are
the calls originating from?

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:02.744
Do I have the right kind of--

00:12:02.744 --> 00:12:04.660
what are the distribution
between the clients?

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:05.880
Is it iOS or Android?

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:07.630
All those kinds of
things you can actually

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:09.577
infer from the analytics itself.

00:12:09.577 --> 00:12:11.410
And last but not the
least, which is, again,

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:13.780
important from a
security point of view

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.810
sometimes and the compliances,
who had the access

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:18.580
and what changes happened.

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:21.330
That kind of auditing capability
is, again, available to you

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:23.400
in the product.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:25.710
All these things give you
the right level of visibility

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:29.970
and the right level of controls
for what you want to do.

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.100
Then comes the reusability
and enforcement.

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:34.920
And this is something, again, we
introduced as a product feature

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.350
about 30 to 45 days back.

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:39.240
And the goal here is
to really give you

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:42.000
a set of tools to take
all the stuff we already

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:45.120
have in terms of
policies and the code

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:47.130
capability we have
in the product,

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:51.050
but package them as reusable
things for your developers.

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:52.840
Why is this important?

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:56.130
A lot of your developers
are just too busy.

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:59.860
They're doing things and they
don't have the time or patience

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:01.570
to learn one more tool.

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:04.300
So the more you make it easy
for them to consume something

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.270
from Apigee, it becomes
easier in their work flow,

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:08.770
and they can go and do it.

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:10.464
Number two, consistency.

00:13:10.464 --> 00:13:12.130
And the reason you
want to be consistent

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:15.970
about this is, primarily,
the developer experience

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:17.350
was consuming the API.

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:20.440
It doesn't think that
you have 10 developer

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:23.080
teams within your
organization and each one

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:25.600
is producing something which
is very different in terms

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:27.040
of an API experience.

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:29.410
And they look at it and
go, you're one company,

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:31.690
how come you're delivering
these APIs as if you're

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:33.310
10 different companies?

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:34.960
That's the other challenge.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:37.180
The last one is enforcement.

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:40.060
In certain cases,
sheer security needs

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:41.730
are that you want
to enforce something

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:43.480
and you don't want
to give a choice.

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:45.680
And that could be because
you have sensitive data,

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:47.800
you could have sensitive
business needs.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:49.930
And there, it's not a
choice for the developer,

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:52.030
it just gets enforced.

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:55.630
Again, it still adds to the
consistency, but in this case,

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:58.360
it's more of a need for you
from a business point of view,

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.030
that you want this
to be enforced.

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:05.110
So when you think about
it in those three aspects,

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:08.350
the reusability and enforcement
becomes very, very important.

00:14:08.350 --> 00:14:11.240
But going back to my starting
premise, which is you

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:15.670
do this with the developer
productivity in mind.

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:17.470
This cannot be a blocker.

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:20.470
This cannot slow the way
you roll things out in terms

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:21.940
of your APIs.

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:24.940
Or it cannot create a friction
when it comes to somebody

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:26.440
consuming your APIs.

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:29.560
So you really are looking at
delivering this reusability

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:32.020
and enforcement
in a fashion which

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:35.950
really does little to slow
things down from your side.

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:37.770
Now, you've heard
me repeatedly talk

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:41.770
about speed, reusability speed,
development, and productivity.

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:44.760
Why is this important?

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:48.210
The classic example today
is that when somebody

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:49.650
needs an API--
let's say somebody

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:52.020
wants to take in a payment API.

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:54.670
A developer wants to take
a payment API, use it

00:14:54.670 --> 00:14:56.960
in their application.

00:14:56.960 --> 00:15:00.470
The starting point is they
just do a Google search.

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:01.850
They look at some APIs.

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:04.040
They go and read up
the documentation.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:06.380
If that doesn't appeal
to them and they

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:08.660
can't make sense of
the documentation

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:10.834
and then the usage of
that API fast enough,

00:15:10.834 --> 00:15:12.500
they're going to move
to something else.

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:13.659
That's one way to do it.

00:15:13.659 --> 00:15:15.950
The other one is they're
going to look at the community

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:19.040
wherever they are, whether
they are hanging around

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:21.750
in Stack Overflow, or they
go to an event like this,

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:23.390
they're going to talk to people.

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:24.110
And guess what?

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:26.870
They're going to ask them, hey,
what payment API you guys use

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:28.130
in your e-commerce site?

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:30.230
Somebody's going
to say, I use this.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:30.870
Why?

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:31.880
It's easy to use.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:34.430
It took me 10 minutes
to integrate that

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:37.490
into my workflow, or
one day and we're done.

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:40.140
That is what the
developer is looking for.

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:42.890
Now, when you produce an API
for whatever the business

00:15:42.890 --> 00:15:46.430
you are in, you're going to
have the same level of scrutiny,

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:47.540
no matter what.

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:49.669
Now, it may not be half
an hour, it may not

00:15:49.669 --> 00:15:51.210
be a 10-minute
benchmark, because you

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.526
may be in a much more
complex business.

00:15:53.526 --> 00:15:55.650
But the speed at which the
developers are consuming

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:59.350
your API is important.

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:02.700
And for you to get there and
improve their experience,

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:05.810
you're going to learn
fine-tune your API,

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:08.330
improve your API constantly.

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:10.444
And if you're not learning
and improving your API,

00:16:10.444 --> 00:16:12.110
you're going to lose
the developer who's

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:15.260
going to consume your API,
so hence the need for you

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:19.370
to have the speed at which you
can learn and improve your API.

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:20.945
So it is not about blind speed.

00:16:20.945 --> 00:16:23.870
It is not about you
trying to race somebody.

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:27.936
It is just the fact that as you
learn, as you improve your API,

00:16:27.936 --> 00:16:29.810
you're going to have a
much better experience

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:31.860
for the developer who's
going to consume it.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:35.060
And that's the reason why you
want speed in your development

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:35.910
cycle.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:39.290
So that's why I keep going
back to developer productivity

00:16:39.290 --> 00:16:41.850
on both sides, your own
developers and the developers

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:43.620
who are going to consume it--

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:44.850
and the reusability.

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:47.970
So with that context,
what I'm going to do

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:50.010
is invite Prithpal
up on stage, who's

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:51.720
going to actually
walk you through,

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:54.810
very specifically, what
we do in the product

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:57.390
to help facilitate all
these three things,

00:16:57.390 --> 00:17:00.270
in terms of security, in terms
of visibility, and in terms

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:02.500
of reusability and enforcement.

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:03.913
Please welcome Prithpal.

