00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:58.440
And there are a dozen tools
available out there for you

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:01.020
to author through OpenAPI Specs.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:04.980
But one of the key advantages
of the OpenAPI Spec in general

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:08.070
is the ability for it
to be language-agnostic.

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:10.540
So that's one way for you to
describe the intent of what

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:13.170
your API is, what
kind of operations

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:15.210
are going to be
supported on the API,

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:20.610
and more importantly, what
kind of capabilities of the API

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:22.140
that are going to be supported.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:23.620
It's very easy to understand.

00:19:23.620 --> 00:19:24.780
There's huge adoption.

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:27.210
As Bala also
mentioned, not only are

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:29.940
we supporting OpenAPI
for some time,

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:32.280
we are a very active
contributing member

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:33.900
of that specification.

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:35.340
So you'll continue to see that.

