00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:09.022
that describes the
user experience.

00:24:09.022 --> 00:24:10.480
And I can guarantee,
many folks are

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:13.647
going to be searching at the
App Store for a newer app.

00:24:13.647 --> 00:24:15.480
Believe it or not, a
very similar experience

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:18.540
is happening in
the industry today.

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:21.390
Bala spoke about some
payment providers.

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:24.970
Pay Easy, Stripe-- they are
dime a dozen payment providers.

00:24:24.970 --> 00:24:26.800
If you do not cut
the friction and make

00:24:26.800 --> 00:24:30.650
your API simple and easy
to use, the app developers

00:24:30.650 --> 00:24:33.500
are going to another
provider very, very quickly.

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:35.440
This is very important.

00:24:35.440 --> 00:24:38.920
So you can see, given
the tools and making

00:24:38.920 --> 00:24:41.740
all those capabilities
available within the product,

00:24:41.740 --> 00:24:44.440
it makes it very simple
for the API developer

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:46.390
to start working with this.

00:24:46.390 --> 00:24:48.280
It doesn't really end here.

00:24:48.280 --> 00:24:51.160
The key proposition from
here and the future which

00:24:51.160 --> 00:24:56.990
is core to Apigee is, great,
I now have an API spec,

00:24:56.990 --> 00:25:00.920
how do I take it from the design
phase to the develop phase?

00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:05.060
Just using that button, I'm able
to actually click and generate

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:07.640
an API proxy.

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:10.500
An API proxy is nothing
but-- think of that

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:12.780
as a message flow,
where you can add

00:25:12.780 --> 00:25:14.980
a bunch of different policies.

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:17.400
So you may have some
APIs on the backend,

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:19.650
which is mentioned out here.

00:25:19.650 --> 00:25:21.570
You see the existing API URL?

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:23.670
That is nothing but
the existing API.

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:24.717
That's a backend API.

00:25:24.717 --> 00:25:25.800
It could be a product API.

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:28.680
It could be an order API.

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:31.140
Using this tooling,
I'm able to create

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:33.510
an API proxy in
which you can add

00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:35.230
certain interesting policies.

00:25:35.230 --> 00:25:38.970
So let's say you have an older
API, but it's not secure.

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:42.630
I can create an API proxy,
which becomes like a facade.

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:44.190
Think of that as a facade.

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:47.220
And I can just add
a security policy.

00:25:47.220 --> 00:25:50.270
And just by using configuration
or indirect constructs,

00:25:50.270 --> 00:25:52.640
and guess what happens,
your API suddenly

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:55.400
is now secure using OAuth.

00:25:55.400 --> 00:25:58.290
I can also add things such
as transformation, et cetera.

00:25:58.290 --> 00:25:59.360
So you get the point.

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:01.070
You may have one, or
two, or three APIs

00:26:01.070 --> 00:26:03.430
that you explored on this
side to your consumers,

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:05.690
but you can manage all
the inconsistencies

00:26:05.690 --> 00:26:08.300
that you may have today by
using some clean consistent

00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:10.130
standards.

00:26:10.130 --> 00:26:13.320
So I can take this
a step forward.

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:16.040
Click on Next.

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:22.671
Next, add certain
choices here, deploy it.

00:26:22.671 --> 00:26:26.170
And at this point in time,
in less than three minutes,

00:26:26.170 --> 00:26:30.769
I've taken an API spec and
generated an API proxy.

00:26:30.769 --> 00:26:32.560
This proxy is deployed
in the testing room.

00:26:32.560 --> 00:26:35.080
And I can try it, I can use it.

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:37.900
Think about the lifecycle
and how collaboration

00:26:37.900 --> 00:26:42.670
could be achieved using some of
the tools that Apigee provides.

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:44.860
So let's switch back
to the slides please.

00:26:48.710 --> 00:26:50.600
So let's talk a little
bit about reusability

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:52.700
because this is a very,
very important aspect.

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:55.520
And one of the key things
which Bala touched on early on

00:26:55.520 --> 00:27:00.890
is, it's not only just a few
ad hoc API developers building

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:04.120
APIs, although many
start that way.

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:07.280
We see we have big
enterprise customers who

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:11.000
have hundreds of API developers
across different business

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:12.987
organizations and
different teams.

00:27:12.987 --> 00:27:14.570
When you're talking
about doing things

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:18.890
at scale, every single action,
every single configuration

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:20.120
counts.

00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:23.220
We have customers who have
well over hundreds of thousands

00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:25.010
of APIs in some instances.

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:27.200
Some have internal APIs.

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:28.970
Some have external
APIs, which are

00:27:28.970 --> 00:27:31.080
exposed to the outside world.

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:34.659
So where does
reusability kick in?

00:27:34.659 --> 00:27:36.700
Some of the common things
we see our customers do

00:27:36.700 --> 00:27:39.860
is, I may have a
very specific way

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:45.020
of solving a specific utility
function within the enterprise.

00:27:45.020 --> 00:27:47.330
Using Apigee Edge,
you can create that

00:27:47.330 --> 00:27:51.860
as a policy which can be reused
across the entire enterprise.

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:55.700
It saves every API
developer that valuable time

00:27:55.700 --> 00:27:57.929
in trying to do
what they need to.

00:27:57.929 --> 00:27:59.720
One of the areas that
I'm going to touch on

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:04.230
in this demo segment is a
feature called shared flows.

00:28:04.230 --> 00:28:07.410
So shared flows is something
which we have released

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:11.090
in the last 40 days or so.

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:14.570
It gives the ability
for API developers

00:28:14.570 --> 00:28:19.160
to be able to share some useful
interaction or design patterns

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:21.290
that they have already sold.

00:28:21.290 --> 00:28:25.340
They can be reused across many
other developers for building

00:28:25.340 --> 00:28:26.090
APIs.

00:28:26.090 --> 00:28:28.850
So in this example, I'm
going to, in the backdrop,

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:32.780
talk about we have many
customers who are integrating.

00:28:32.780 --> 00:28:35.570
And I'm sure many
of you guys out here

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:36.832
have very similar needs.

00:28:36.832 --> 00:28:39.290
Let's say there's an example
of someone trying to integrate

00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:41.200
with Salesforce APIs.

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:43.190
It could be any other
cloud API provider,

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:45.430
for that matter of fact.

00:28:45.430 --> 00:28:49.810
There are some very
simple, yet important steps

00:28:49.810 --> 00:28:53.490
that you have to do to get
access to the sales force APIs.

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:54.960
And those three things are--

00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:57.870
you need to have a user
ID, an account out there.

00:28:57.870 --> 00:29:00.760
You need to call
a token endpoint

00:29:00.760 --> 00:29:03.370
to get some OAuth token.

00:29:03.370 --> 00:29:05.880
And once you have that,
then you can make a call

00:29:05.880 --> 00:29:09.130
to the account API, the
case, the opportunity,

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:11.170
so on and so forth.

00:29:11.170 --> 00:29:15.010
Think about, for a
second, that you and some

00:29:15.010 --> 00:29:17.830
of the other developers are
tasked with building out

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:21.797
some integration internally with
some of those sales force APIs.

00:29:21.797 --> 00:29:23.380
There's a very common
pattern that you

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:25.720
have to use across
all the interactions.

00:29:25.720 --> 00:29:29.260
So let's take a look at how,
using the shared flow feature,

00:29:29.260 --> 00:29:33.490
you can provide those kinds
of mechanics of reusability

00:29:33.490 --> 00:29:35.642
within the enterprise.

00:29:35.642 --> 00:29:36.850
Let's go to the demo, please.

00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:44.020
All right, so short of using
the sales for the APIs,

00:29:44.020 --> 00:29:47.350
in this case I'm actually
going to use the API from one

00:29:47.350 --> 00:29:50.320
of the Apigee components
called the API BaaS, or Backend

00:29:50.320 --> 00:29:52.080
as a Service.

00:29:52.080 --> 00:29:55.510
API BaaS is a component
of the Apigee Stack

00:29:55.510 --> 00:29:59.320
which gives you the ability
to have data collection.

00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:03.430
So you can very quickly, on the
fly, create a data collection.

00:30:03.430 --> 00:30:05.770
So you can say, here is an
employee data collection.

00:30:05.770 --> 00:30:07.660
I can add some records to it.

00:30:07.660 --> 00:30:09.700
It is native JSON-based.

00:30:09.700 --> 00:30:12.490
And without doing anything, you
have an API endpoint to that--

00:30:12.490 --> 00:30:15.630
so very useful for if you want
to rapidly just prioritize

00:30:15.630 --> 00:30:16.774
something.

00:30:16.774 --> 00:30:18.190
In some cases,
customers are using

00:30:18.190 --> 00:30:20.680
this to support their catalogs.

00:30:20.680 --> 00:30:22.870
In some cases, preferences,
so on and so forth.

00:30:22.870 --> 00:30:26.480
So I'm going to use that
as a backdrop for this API.

00:30:26.480 --> 00:30:29.050
As I mentioned, it
exposes an API endpoint.

00:30:29.050 --> 00:30:31.930
So let's take a look at--

00:30:31.930 --> 00:30:35.280
just keep your watch out
on this URL out here.

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:38.500
I'll expand this a little bit.

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:40.030
So that's the stage.

00:30:40.030 --> 00:30:42.900
So you can see the URL
endpoint on the top right.

00:30:42.900 --> 00:30:45.820
Now, this endpoint
and the employees

00:30:45.820 --> 00:30:47.485
collection that you can see--

00:30:47.485 --> 00:30:50.630
I'm from the LA area, so you
can see a slight Hollywood

00:30:50.630 --> 00:30:53.140
preference that I have out
here with the employees

00:30:53.140 --> 00:30:55.100
in my employees collection.

00:30:55.100 --> 00:30:58.620
So what I'm going to do
out here is, let's quickly

00:30:58.620 --> 00:31:05.116
try to call this the
employees collection.

00:31:05.116 --> 00:31:06.490
You can see it's
an API endpoint.

00:31:06.490 --> 00:31:08.350
So again, I'm just
picking up this

00:31:08.350 --> 00:31:10.460
as the backdrop for my example.

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:13.240
This could be the sales for
the API, and any other cloud

00:31:13.240 --> 00:31:15.850
provider API, for
that matter of fact.

00:31:15.850 --> 00:31:20.880
So when I call this,
you can see that this

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:24.014
