00:33:04.740 --> 00:33:06.174
So I'll call out certain things.

00:33:06.174 --> 00:33:07.590
What you're really
seeing out here

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:09.900
is the anatomy of an API proxy.

00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:12.310
This is how an API
proxy looks like.

00:33:12.310 --> 00:33:16.240
So I happen to be in the
API Proxy Editor View.

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:19.680
And in this case, you can
see that I have some caching

00:33:19.680 --> 00:33:21.690
policies which I've used.

00:33:21.690 --> 00:33:25.080
We actually happen to have 30
out-of-the-box policies to do

00:33:25.080 --> 00:33:27.060
a variety of different
kinds of things.

00:33:27.060 --> 00:33:29.260
So you can do
traffic management.

00:33:29.260 --> 00:33:33.920
You can do security, which
I'll cover in the next piece.

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:35.600
You can do threat protection.

00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:36.800
You can do mediation.

00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:38.540
And more importantly,
the platform

00:33:38.540 --> 00:33:42.640
is extensible, which means you
can alter your own policies.

00:33:42.640 --> 00:33:46.800
So in this case, in the
Employees API proxy,

00:33:46.800 --> 00:33:49.350
I had three things
that I'm doing.

00:33:49.350 --> 00:33:54.800
I have to make a call to a
