00:34:03.630 --> 00:34:05.640
So I make a call with
some credentials,

00:34:05.640 --> 00:34:07.580
I get an access
token, because we

00:34:07.580 --> 00:34:10.110
are trying to access the
stage environment, which

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:12.600
requires some
authorization before I

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:15.120
can access any dataset.

00:34:15.120 --> 00:34:17.820
And then, that header
needs to be set.

00:34:17.820 --> 00:34:19.630
So just imagine
this for a second,

00:34:19.630 --> 00:34:22.239
these three activities
have to be done,

00:34:22.239 --> 00:34:24.929
no matter whether I'm trying
to access the employees

00:34:24.929 --> 00:34:27.840
collection, or an
activities collection,

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:29.610
or a hotels dataset,
or whatever dataset

00:34:29.610 --> 00:34:32.170
that you've created out there.

00:34:32.170 --> 00:34:37.416
So in this case, we go to this
thing called a shared flow.

00:34:37.416 --> 00:34:41.179
So in the shared
flow, in this area,

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:42.949
you can go create shared flow.

00:34:42.949 --> 00:34:46.170
Then shared flows are nothing
but a very simple construct.

00:34:46.170 --> 00:34:49.010
Think of that as an API
proxy at some level,

00:34:49.010 --> 00:34:51.530
but it's kind of
a flow which can

00:34:51.530 --> 00:34:54.949
contain policies which can
be referenced by multiple API

00:34:54.949 --> 00:34:56.226
proxies.

00:34:56.226 --> 00:34:58.100
So just play along with
me for a few seconds.

00:34:58.100 --> 00:34:59.450
It'll become very clear.

00:34:59.450 --> 00:35:01.970
So in this case,
I know that I need

00:35:01.970 --> 00:35:05.330
to access those three
policies we just discussed--

00:35:05.330 --> 00:35:09.240
make a call to API BaaS token
endpoint, get the old token

00:35:09.240 --> 00:35:11.055
and set it in the
header, whether I'm

00:35:11.055 --> 00:35:12.680
trying to call the
employees collection

00:35:12.680 --> 00:35:14.600
or any other collection.

00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:16.940
So let's go into
the shared flow.

00:35:16.940 --> 00:35:23.360
And in this shared flow, if
I click on the Develop tab,

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:27.060
you can see that I've
copied the exact same three

00:35:27.060 --> 00:35:29.880
policies in here.

00:35:29.880 --> 00:35:32.120
I just showed you Revision
1 of the API proxy.

00:35:32.120 --> 00:35:33.870
And I'm just saying,
all right, let me now

00:35:33.870 --> 00:35:36.180
move them into a shared
flow, because I know,

00:35:36.180 --> 00:35:39.661
once I'm solving this for
my employees API proxy,

00:35:39.661 --> 00:35:41.910
this functionality is going
to be needed by developers

00:35:41.910 --> 00:35:44.790
across many other
backend collections,

00:35:44.790 --> 00:35:47.940
and we can solve this
once and reuse that

00:35:47.940 --> 00:35:49.350
across many implementations.

00:35:49.350 --> 00:35:51.460
So I have a shared
flow, and it's

00:35:51.460 --> 00:35:58.460
deployed both to the test
and the product environment.

00:35:58.460 --> 00:36:01.350
I'm going back to
the API proxies.

00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:06.770
I now have the
employees API proxy.

00:36:06.770 --> 00:36:10.300
And I'll switch to Revision
3, which is out here.

00:36:10.300 --> 00:36:12.400
And in the Develop
tab now, you can

00:36:12.400 --> 00:36:15.040
see that I accessed
that using something

00:36:15.040 --> 00:36:19.350
called as a flow callout.

00:36:19.350 --> 00:36:20.940
So in this case,
all I have done is

00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:24.090
I've shown you the shared flow
with those three policies.

00:36:24.090 --> 00:36:27.030
And in this case, I'm just
making a call to that.

00:36:27.030 --> 00:36:29.864
So think about all the
other developers who

00:36:29.864 --> 00:36:32.280
are going to be now needing
access to either the employees

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:33.946
collection or any of
the data collection

00:36:33.946 --> 00:36:37.830
that you build out there, can
just reuse the exact same flow

00:36:37.830 --> 00:36:39.551
across all the APIs.

00:36:39.551 --> 00:36:40.800
Let's try and test this thing.

00:36:43.407 --> 00:36:44.990
One of the interesting
things that I'm

00:36:44.990 --> 00:36:47.406
sure you're going to see this
thing in some other sessions

00:36:47.406 --> 00:36:51.610
as well, is the ability
to look at what's

00:36:51.610 --> 00:36:55.180
going on when an API
proxy is being tested out.

00:36:55.180 --> 00:36:59.260
It's very useful for some
customers and developers who

00:36:59.260 --> 00:37:02.080
are actually building APIs and
want to be able to quickly test

00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:04.090
out some of these things.

00:37:04.090 --> 00:37:07.600
You come and put the API
proxy in a trace mode.

00:37:07.600 --> 00:37:12.390
And now, I'm going to
call not this endpoint,

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:14.990
but the employees endpoint out.

00:37:14.990 --> 00:37:17.180
Here this is a new
API endpoint which

00:37:17.180 --> 00:37:19.790
is enabled by the API proxy.

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:22.630
So let's call this.

00:37:22.630 --> 00:37:27.400
And you can see that I get the
data back from the collection.

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:29.120
So we did a few things.

00:37:29.120 --> 00:37:30.754
I showed you what
a shared flow is.

00:37:30.754 --> 00:37:32.170
I showed you what
kind of policies

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:34.420
you can attach out there.

00:37:34.420 --> 00:37:36.660
And then I also showed
how that shared flow

00:37:36.660 --> 00:37:39.810
can be invoked from one
or multiple API proxies.

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:41.730
So that's a great
way for you to be

00:37:41.730 --> 00:37:44.300
able to distribute some
of the common things

00:37:44.300 --> 00:37:47.820
that you're doing across some
backend third party systems,

00:37:47.820 --> 00:37:52.780
and then leverage that in
your development initiatives.

00:37:52.780 --> 00:37:53.680
Awesome.

00:37:53.680 --> 00:37:55.961
So now, let's take
it to the next level.

00:37:55.961 --> 00:37:57.460
Can we switch to
the slides, please?

00:38:02.060 --> 00:38:04.660
So Bala spoke about
one very key thing.

00:38:04.660 --> 00:38:07.690
As you start to scale
out your API program,

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:11.320
as you have not one,
10, but hundreds,

00:38:11.320 --> 00:38:14.350
and more and more
APIs, it becomes

00:38:14.350 --> 00:38:17.650
very important to
have some very, very

00:38:17.650 --> 00:38:21.860
horizontal cross-cutting
policies enforced.

00:38:21.860 --> 00:38:25.140
So what do I mean by that?

00:38:25.140 --> 00:38:28.200
It's very important to
leverage your API platform

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:32.160
to be agile so you can really
roll out APIs very rapidly.

00:38:32.160 --> 00:38:34.040
That's important.

00:38:34.040 --> 00:38:37.810
That's what an API developer or
an API provider is tasked with.

00:38:37.810 --> 00:38:40.280
They just want to roll
out APIs very quickly.

00:38:40.280 --> 00:38:44.660
However, from a security and
a compliance perspective,

00:38:44.660 --> 00:38:46.580
those teams want
to ensure that when

00:38:46.580 --> 00:38:50.420
APIs make it into
production, they are secure.

00:38:50.420 --> 00:38:53.330
They cannot afford to delegate
the responsibility and assume

00:38:53.330 --> 00:38:56.360
and trust that the developers
are going to do that.

00:38:56.360 --> 00:38:58.124
With all good
intentions, developers

00:38:58.124 --> 00:38:59.540
will try and do
what they need to,

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:01.430
but there are
common requirements

00:39:01.430 --> 00:39:04.160
such as I need to have
consistent security across all

00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:05.860
my APIs.

00:39:05.860 --> 00:39:08.320
That's something that the
security and the compliance

00:39:08.320 --> 00:39:14.170
team needs to have the ability
at their level to enforce it.

00:39:14.170 --> 00:39:15.900
The other thing is logging.

00:39:15.900 --> 00:39:18.700
Let's say there's a requirement
from an audit perspective.

00:39:18.700 --> 00:39:21.250
And many of our customers do--
in fact, many of my customers

00:39:21.250 --> 00:39:28.480
that we talk to say, because
APIs are the face to the world

00:39:28.480 --> 00:39:31.630
digitally, and they are now
enabling many interactions

00:39:31.630 --> 00:39:35.560
through APIs, that means
every single interaction which

00:39:35.560 --> 00:39:40.330
happens on that channel has to
be recorded, has to be audited,

00:39:40.330 --> 00:39:42.430
has to be logged somewhere.

00:39:42.430 --> 00:39:45.130
And many of our customers
use several different kinds

00:39:45.130 --> 00:39:52.550
of enterprise logging
systems such as Splunk, Sumo,

00:39:52.550 --> 00:39:54.780
Loggly, and a few others.

00:39:54.780 --> 00:39:58.690
So those aspects
become a very key part

00:39:58.690 --> 00:40:01.120
of ensuring that
you have consistency

00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:03.580
in terms of security,
consistency in terms

00:40:03.580 --> 00:40:05.950
of logging and auditing.

00:40:05.950 --> 00:40:09.790
And in this section, I'm going
to show you one feature called

00:40:09.790 --> 00:40:11.170
the flow hooks.

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:14.680
It's a great mechanism
for all the administrators

00:40:14.680 --> 00:40:17.290
and the security
and compliance folks

00:40:17.290 --> 00:40:19.750
who are leveraging
the Apigee platform

00:40:19.750 --> 00:40:23.440
to be able to enforce
that using a few clicks.

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:25.630
So we'll talk about
how flow hooks work

00:40:25.630 --> 00:40:28.510
and how you can use that feature
to provide the same benefits

00:40:28.510 --> 00:40:30.570
in your enterprise as well.

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:33.442
Can you switch to
the demo, please?

00:40:37.680 --> 00:40:40.120
So we're back out here.

00:40:40.120 --> 00:40:43.220
Now, let's play this thing
a little bit forward.

00:40:43.220 --> 00:40:47.110
So you saw a very simple
employees API proxy

00:40:47.110 --> 00:40:49.480
which gives me access
to some backend data.

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:50.840
I can do some caching.

00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:55.920
And I'm off building
an app on top of it.

00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:59.310
Although this is
OK for me to have

00:40:59.310 --> 00:41:03.940
an API like this
in development, you

00:41:03.940 --> 00:41:09.430
can see that there is no API
key, no API security that

00:41:09.430 --> 00:41:11.950
was needed to call this API.

00:41:11.950 --> 00:41:14.740
So if I'm a developer
and this is a public URL,

00:41:14.740 --> 00:41:16.450
you could try it right now.

00:41:16.450 --> 00:41:18.880
You can keep hitting
this endpoint,

00:41:18.880 --> 00:41:22.330
and it will give
you the API data.

00:41:22.330 --> 00:41:25.150
And although that's OK when
you're developing and testing

00:41:25.150 --> 00:41:28.550
it, it's not OK when
you go to production.

00:41:28.550 --> 00:41:31.240
The security team has
the responsibility there.

00:41:31.240 --> 00:41:33.850
So they want to
ensure that if it

00:41:33.850 --> 00:41:38.605
goes into production,
at a minimum that API

00:41:38.605 --> 00:41:41.950
is protected using an API key.

00:41:41.950 --> 00:41:44.580
Sure, they can have more
exaggerated requirements,

00:41:44.580 --> 00:41:47.460
where it needs to have
an OAuth protection.

00:41:47.460 --> 00:41:49.590
I need to have some threat
protection, some traffic

00:41:49.590 --> 00:41:50.780
management.

00:41:50.780 --> 00:41:53.490
So let's use the simple example
of let's try and protect

00:41:53.490 --> 00:41:57.850
this API, but the ops
and the security team

00:41:57.850 --> 00:41:59.890
want to be very,
very clear of not

00:41:59.890 --> 00:42:02.579
delegating the responsibility,
and relying that the developers

00:42:02.579 --> 00:42:03.370
are going to do it.

00:42:03.370 --> 00:42:04.911
Because we have a
bunch of developers

00:42:04.911 --> 00:42:07.390
who are off building
their APIs, but production

00:42:07.390 --> 00:42:09.520
is the environment
that they control.

00:42:09.520 --> 00:42:12.850
So let's show you how the flow
hook feature kind of enables

00:42:12.850 --> 00:42:14.970
that.

00:42:14.970 --> 00:42:20.660
So as an example, I have this
API proxy deployed in the fraud

00:42:20.660 --> 00:42:23.240
environment as well.

00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:24.220
Let's just call this--

00:42:24.220 --> 00:42:30.580
I'm going to change the URL
to now say demo5-product.

00:42:30.580 --> 00:42:33.670
And let me try to
call this thing.

00:42:33.670 --> 00:42:38.430
Right away you can see
that I need an API key.

00:42:38.430 --> 00:42:40.260
How did this happen?

00:42:40.260 --> 00:42:43.670
It's the exact same API
proxy which the developer

00:42:43.670 --> 00:42:46.710
built and has deployed it.

00:42:46.710 --> 00:42:49.780
But something changed in
the production environment.

00:42:49.780 --> 00:42:54.510
Let's take a peek on
how that happened.

00:42:54.510 --> 00:42:58.830
So if I go back to the
Employees API proxy,

00:42:58.830 --> 00:43:01.065
I still have those two
things-- caching responses

00:43:01.065 --> 00:43:05.220
returning from there if you
can, or make those three policy

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:08.539
calls to access
the backend system.

00:43:08.539 --> 00:43:09.830
Here is where the magic starts.

00:43:12.530 --> 00:43:18.490
In this Admin tab, when
I click on Environments,

00:43:18.490 --> 00:43:21.740
I have a few key things
which show up here.

00:43:21.740 --> 00:43:24.340
I'll try and zoom
in a little bit.

00:43:24.340 --> 00:43:27.370
The one that I really
want to share out here

00:43:27.370 --> 00:43:28.970
is the flow hooks.

00:43:28.970 --> 00:43:31.780
So flow hooks is a
very simple mechanism

00:43:31.780 --> 00:43:38.330
to say that I have
four entry points.

00:43:38.330 --> 00:43:40.940
So think about an API proxy.

00:43:40.940 --> 00:43:44.990
As I mentioned, an API proxy
is nothing but a facade.

00:43:44.990 --> 00:43:47.270
If you think of this as
your right-hand side,

00:43:47.270 --> 00:43:50.750
or my left-hand side, and think
of this as being your employees

00:43:50.750 --> 00:43:54.500
backend endpoint or
your third party APIs,

00:43:54.500 --> 00:43:58.520
on the right-hand side, or to
your left, is the API consumer.

00:43:58.520 --> 00:44:01.940
In the middle is the API proxy.

00:44:01.940 --> 00:44:04.550
That's where any call
which comes from here

00:44:04.550 --> 00:44:08.586
gets intercepted and gets passed
on to the right-hand side.

00:44:08.586 --> 00:44:10.460
So there are four key
touch points out there,

00:44:10.460 --> 00:44:12.530
or hooks, if you would.

00:44:12.530 --> 00:44:15.230
One is the pre-proxy,
the pre-target,

00:44:15.230 --> 00:44:18.170
and the post-target,
and the post-proxy.

00:44:18.170 --> 00:44:20.360
In a very simple way,
what that means is,

00:44:20.360 --> 00:44:23.810
do some checks on this side
before I even go there.

00:44:23.810 --> 00:44:26.210
And do some checks on this
side before we actually

00:44:26.210 --> 00:44:29.150
send them to the backend.

00:44:29.150 --> 00:44:32.060
So in this case, in
the test environment,

00:44:32.060 --> 00:44:34.550
I do not have anything out here.

00:44:34.550 --> 00:44:37.460
That's why when I'm making
a call to the employee API

00:44:37.460 --> 00:44:41.300
proxy in the test environment,
it works flawlessly

00:44:41.300 --> 00:44:44.330
without any API key.

00:44:44.330 --> 00:44:50.120
But let's go switch this
to the prod environment.

00:44:50.120 --> 00:44:53.885
Once I change the environment
from test to prod--

00:44:53.885 --> 00:44:57.560
aha, you can see that there's
a common security shared

00:44:57.560 --> 00:45:03.429
flow which had been enabled
at the pre-proxy flow hook.

00:45:03.429 --> 00:45:05.720
This is something which the
security and the compliance

00:45:05.720 --> 00:45:07.340
team can easily affect.

00:45:07.340 --> 00:45:10.550
They can go in out
here and put all kinds

00:45:10.550 --> 00:45:13.310
of things, all
kinds of shared flow

00:45:13.310 --> 00:45:15.080
for the different elements.

00:45:15.080 --> 00:45:19.529
So in this case, we use a
common security shared flow.

00:45:19.529 --> 00:45:21.070
Let's go take a look
at what that is.

00:45:23.776 --> 00:45:30.810
If I go back to the shared flow,
in the common security shared

00:45:30.810 --> 00:45:34.230
flow, which is only deployed
to the prod environment,

00:45:34.230 --> 00:45:37.170
and hence, you could see
that the API was protected

00:45:37.170 --> 00:45:40.050
by default without the
developer having to do anything

00:45:40.050 --> 00:45:42.410
in the prod environment.

00:45:42.410 --> 00:45:49.520
And in this case, there
is a verify API key policy

00:45:49.520 --> 00:45:59.440
in there, which is expecting
an API key in the request

00:45:59.440 --> 00:46:02.040
parameter.

00:46:02.040 --> 00:46:04.680
So just by simple
clicks, we were

00:46:04.680 --> 00:46:08.790
able to kind of put this
power in the hands of the ops

00:46:08.790 --> 00:46:10.950
team, the security and
the compliance teams,

00:46:10.950 --> 00:46:14.040
to be able to ensure
that APIs are protected.

00:46:14.040 --> 00:46:17.850
And you can expand this thing
to saying, in this shared flow,

00:46:17.850 --> 00:46:20.400
or in some other ones
at different levels,

00:46:20.400 --> 00:46:22.960
you can actually have
method logging policy.

00:46:22.960 --> 00:46:26.280
So method logging policy is an
example of an out-of-the-box

00:46:26.280 --> 00:46:28.990
policy which can be used to
log to any enterprise logging

00:46:28.990 --> 00:46:29.490
system.

00:46:33.940 --> 00:46:41.520
So now, in this case, let's
switch back to the Postman

00:46:41.520 --> 00:46:43.339
client that I've been using.

00:46:43.339 --> 00:46:45.630
And I'll actually change the
collection that I'm using,

00:46:45.630 --> 00:46:47.750
or the endpoint.

00:46:47.750 --> 00:46:51.200
I'm going to call
the prod endpoint.

00:46:55.070 --> 00:46:59.980
But in this case, I'm going
to actually supply an API key.

00:46:59.980 --> 00:47:02.420
So an API key, in
this case, what

00:47:02.420 --> 00:47:07.260
generated through Apigee Edge,
in which we say, great, someone

00:47:07.260 --> 00:47:10.090
needs the easy
access to the API,

00:47:10.090 --> 00:47:12.190
you can go and
generate an API key

00:47:12.190 --> 00:47:14.350
which can be shared
with your consumer.

00:47:14.350 --> 00:47:16.450
It serves dual purposes.

00:47:16.450 --> 00:47:20.080
Not only it provides
a step of security

00:47:20.080 --> 00:47:23.180
so that the API can only be
called when an API key gets

00:47:23.180 --> 00:47:28.580
passed in, but it also serves
as an excellent identification

00:47:28.580 --> 00:47:31.530
mechanism, where
if you're thinking

00:47:31.530 --> 00:47:33.900
about a bunch of
partners or consumers

00:47:33.900 --> 00:47:36.510
who are calling your
APIs, As you then

00:47:36.510 --> 00:47:39.660
have a very great insight into
who is actually the biggest

00:47:39.660 --> 00:47:42.750
adopter of the APIs, something
which product managers care

00:47:42.750 --> 00:47:43.830
very deeply about.

00:47:43.830 --> 00:47:47.549
If you're thinking of running
your business using APIs,

00:47:47.549 --> 00:47:49.590
that kind of mechanics
play a very important role

00:47:49.590 --> 00:47:52.560
in figuring out who is actually
a big user of the APIs.

00:47:52.560 --> 00:47:55.790
So in this case, I'm
going to pass the API key.

00:47:58.320 --> 00:47:59.820
And you can see
that in production I

00:47:59.820 --> 00:48:07.080
can now start accessing the
APIs as I was in development.

00:48:07.080 --> 00:48:09.440
Makes sense?

00:48:09.440 --> 00:48:12.250
And if I go back to
trying to call this API

00:48:12.250 --> 00:48:14.630
endpoint in production
without an API key,

00:48:14.630 --> 00:48:17.930
it will consistently fail.

00:48:17.930 --> 00:48:21.770
Test, I can keep calling
it multiple times--

00:48:21.770 --> 00:48:23.996
whoops.

00:48:23.996 --> 00:48:25.912
Change this to test.

00:48:30.790 --> 00:48:34.230
So let's switch
back to the slides.

00:48:34.230 --> 00:48:34.730
Great.

00:48:34.730 --> 00:48:37.660
So we took a look
at three themes.

00:48:37.660 --> 00:48:39.500
We spoke about consistency.

00:48:39.500 --> 00:48:42.590
We spoke about how you can
use the OpenAPI Editor to be

00:48:42.590 --> 00:48:46.220
able to have collaboration,
have some consistency in how

00:48:46.220 --> 00:48:48.080
you define the APIs.

00:48:48.080 --> 00:48:49.940
We looked at some
features of the product

00:48:49.940 --> 00:48:54.050
in the area of reusability,
which was all about

00:48:54.050 --> 00:48:56.035
I'm building up
API to scale, I'm

00:48:56.035 --> 00:48:58.160
having some common capabilities
and functionalities

00:48:58.160 --> 00:49:01.010
that I want to share
across my enterprise.

00:49:01.010 --> 00:49:03.640
The shared flow is a great
mechanism to do that.

00:49:03.640 --> 00:49:05.580
And lastly, we spoke
about enforcement.

00:49:05.580 --> 00:49:09.210
And you looked at how
we could use flow hooks

00:49:09.210 --> 00:49:13.290
to be able to enforce some
very common security, logging,

00:49:13.290 --> 00:49:15.480
and compliance
requirements using

00:49:15.480 --> 00:49:18.020
configuration-oriented
constructs.

00:49:18.020 --> 00:49:20.990
So I'm going to turn it over
back to Bala at this point

00:49:20.990 --> 00:49:24.550
to take us to close.

00:49:24.550 --> 00:49:26.526
BALA KASIVISWANATHAN:
Thank you, Prithpal.

00:49:26.526 --> 00:49:28.820
So hopefully the demo
was very illustrative

00:49:28.820 --> 00:49:32.950
of the things we talked about,
in terms of when we think about

00:49:32.950 --> 00:49:35.770
security, reusability,
and enforcement,

00:49:35.770 --> 00:49:38.110
and how do we achieve
that in the product.

00:49:38.110 --> 00:49:40.090
And going back to the
summary view here,

00:49:40.090 --> 00:49:42.550
the reason we care
about this deeply is we

00:49:42.550 --> 00:49:45.870
want the API platform
to be all about getting

00:49:45.870 --> 00:49:47.980
your adoption for your APIs.

00:49:47.980 --> 00:49:50.090
If you have great APIs
and nobody's using them,

00:49:50.090 --> 00:49:51.371
it's kind of a waste.

00:49:51.371 --> 00:49:53.745
So we want to make sure that
all your internal developers

00:49:53.745 --> 00:49:55.828
and external developers
are able to take advantage

00:49:55.828 --> 00:49:59.470
of those APIs, you're
able to scale and serve

00:49:59.470 --> 00:50:02.994
those developers very, very well
and applications really well.

00:50:02.994 --> 00:50:04.660
And the last one is,
of course, that you

00:50:04.660 --> 00:50:06.400
want to make sure
that you still have

00:50:06.400 --> 00:50:12.480
a lot of control and security
